Counters and a sysctl for what code signatures with 16 KiB hash pages cost on 4 KiB pages.

A code directory with 16 KiB hash pages is authenticated when its signature is added (cs_native4k_prepare,
native-4k-signature-hash-cache.patch): the image is read up to its code limit, every 16 KiB block is hashed against the
code directory and every 4 KiB page of it again, and the derived hashes stay wired for as long as the vnode keeps the
blob. The thread that adds the signature waits for all of it: the exec of a main binary, dyld for a library or bundle,
the first thread of launchd for the files of the dyld shared cache.

This changes nothing that is read, accepted or refused, and adds:
- by class (exec; library; large, above the uncached size: the shared cache files and any other image that large):
  blobs, bytes and the time of the prepare;
- the wired bytes of derived hashes held now and at most, the blobs released and refused, and the longest single
  prepare with its file name;
- the 16 KiB blocks that had a page validated at least once (one bit per block in a map the blob owns, set where
  cs_validate_hash serves a derived hash): against the blocks hashed at load, this is what authenticating a block at
  its first page validation would have read;
- `sysctl vm.cs_native4k_stats`, one line of text, and cs_native4k_stats_text() for the board log.
